 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Performance:** V4D achieves state-of-the-art performance on synthetic dynamic scenes (e.g., PSNR 33.72, SSIM 0.98 on datasets [23]) and competitive performance on real dynamic scenes [S1]. It outperforms D-NeRF, NDVG, and TiNeuVox in PSNR/SSIM [S1].
-*   **Training Time:** Training requires 250k iterations for datasets [23] and 100k for datasets [21]. It has a longer training time than canonical-space methods (like D-NeRF) because it lacks canonical space adaptation and relies on TV loss to propagate temporal information, requiring more iterations [S1].
-*   **Inference Time:** V4D has shorter inference time than TiNeuVox (0.48s vs 1.42s on A100) because it does not use an MLP-based sub-network to predict position offsets for each moment [S1].
-*   **Memory:** Uses two $160^3 \times 12$ voxel grids and five $33^3$ LUTs. This is more memory-intensive than compact MLP representations but allows for faster inference compared to methods requiring per-frame offset prediction [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** No canonical space required; plug-and-play LUT refinement; high-quality dynamic novel view synthesis from single-view video [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Longer training time compared to canonical-space methods; potential for over-smoothing if TV loss is not balanced with CPE/LUT refinement [S1].
+*   **Time and Memory Complexity:** Training requires 250k iterations for datasets [23] and 100k for datasets [21] [S1]. Memory usage consists of two $160^3 \times 12$ voxel grids and five $33^3$ LUTs [S1].
+*   **Measured Performance:** V4D achieves state-of-the-art performance on synthetic dynamic scenes (e.g., PSNR 33.72, SSIM 0.98 on datasets [23]) and competitive performance on real dynamic scenes [S1]. It outperforms D-NeRF, NDVG, and TiNeuVox in PSNR/SSIM [S1].
+*   **Scaling:** The method scales to 4K resolution in related real-time variants but standard V4D focuses on single-view dynamic scene synthesis [S1].
+*   **Strengths and Limitations:** Strengths include no canonical space requirement, plug-and-play LUT refinement, and high-quality dynamic novel view synthesis from single-view video [S1]. Limitations include longer training time compared to canonical-space methods and potential for over-smoothing if TV loss is not balanced with CPE/LUT refinement [S1].
+*   **When to Use:** Use V4D when synthesizing novel views of dynamic scenes from single-view video without requiring a canonical space or multi-view constraints [S1]. Avoid it when faster training convergence is prioritized over inference speed or when multi-view inputs are available [S1].
 
